namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        public void PUT_GetContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int index)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index < arrList.Count && index >= 0);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_index = index;

            int New_Ret = (int)(arrList[index]);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            bool New_containsNewRet = arrList.Contains(New_Ret);

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_index", Old_index);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$New_ret", New_Ret);

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_containsNewRet", New_containsNewRet);

            Debug.Assert(New_containsNewRet); // @@@ the array contains New_Ret
        }
    }
}
```

This code will add an assert statement at the end of the method to verify New_containsNewRet is true, exactly corresponding to the comment. Please note that the "Debug" and "AssumePrecondition" namespace may not be imported in your project for the assert and the assumption to compile. You would need to add those using statements based on your actual setup. Remember that you will need to uncomment this code when you want it to be active.--------------------Here are the C# code snippets that output assert statements corresponding to the "@@@" comments found in the provided C# code. 

```cs
Debug.Assert(New_containsNewRet); // @@@ the array contains New_Ret 
```

Ensure you have `System.Diagnostics` namespace imported before running this assertion. 

Note: Debug.Assert is for checking invariant conditions, so this is a way to check an equality (New_containsNewRet should be true if the array contains New_Ret). If the assertion fails, the program will stop at the line where Debug.Assert is and an exception will pop-up containing the assert message. Thus, you should carefully use Assertions to ensure the correctness of your program as they may behave differently than regular expressions or output statements in a debugging session.--------------------The code you posted does not contain any comments that start with "@@@", so there's nothing to output. However, for any case, if we had such a comment in the code and we were looking to extract the line in which it occurs, this would be a solution:

```cs
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;
using System;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        string text = @"
            // other code...
            // @@@ the array contains New_Ret 
            // ...other code
        ";

        Match match = Regex.Match(text, @"// @@@ (.*)");
        if (match.Success)
        {
            Console.WriteLine("Found: " + match.Groups[1]);
        }
        else
        {
            Console.WriteLine("No match found");
        }
    }
}
```
In this code, the Regex.Match method searches the text for a comment that starts with "@@@". The parentheses around "(.*?)" mean "group everything we find that is not a line break". The Console.WriteLine will then output the text after "@@@". If no such comment is found, it will print "No match found".--------------------The following C# code generates the corresponding assert statements for your desired comments in the code:

