Here is the whole code with the assert statements.

```cs
namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        public void PUT_GetContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int index)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index < arrList.Count && index >= 0);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_index = index;

            int New_Ret = (int)(arrList[index]);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            bool New_containsNewRet = arrList.Contains(New_Ret);

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_index", Old_index);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$New_ret", New_Ret);

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_containsNewRet", New_containsNewRet);

            Debug.Assert(New_containsNewRet, "The array does not contain New_Ret");
        }
    }
}
```
It's important to note that Debug.Assert should be used for development and testing purposes and never used in a production environment. In test methods, exceptions should be used instead to indicate a situation that shouldn't actually happen given the correct inputs.--------------------This code doesn't seem to use Debug.Assert. The code seems to just verify that an array list contains a certain element which is done naturally in csharp. The statement for verification is placed with comments (for clarification). However, some other parts of the code are also written in comments with an @@@ symbol, but it isn't directly tied to the current code logic. 

This means that these comments could be written outside of the given csharp code, and it seems to be added in this context due to the given context of the "@@@" symbol comments.

